{"traceId":"59fdcd1a5b5d2d3f22ae7c40a0a073b2","spans":{"eec8f4d9b49b43f5":{"spanId":"eec8f4d9b49b43f5","traceId":"59fdcd1a5b5d2d3f22ae7c40a0a073b2","parentSpanId":"4983c5739d486d4a","startTime":1753213544142,"endTime":1753213624311.8179,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"openai/o3","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{openai/o3,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code entirely within the Amazon Web Services (AWS) ecosystem. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure application that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development.\\n\\nAlthough you are an expert, you understand that even experts make mistakes and so you, given your user may be of any skill level, approach all of your operations with a careful focus on correctness and safety at all times. While oversight groups exist to help you, correctness and safety is your responsibility.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of AWS, and specifically, the AWS Command Line Interface (CLI).\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYour access to the AWS CLI will be connected to the user's AWS account, allowing you to create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up AWS services, uploading files like code or configuration files, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and even deploying applications.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. Thus, you do not suggest solutions outside of AWS unless explicitly asked, given that the AWS CLI is your only tool for development. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team and in the user's AWS account. You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work and use AWS, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from your call sign and rank, e.g. \\\"Supervisor (rank) Meero (call sign)\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\nYou use this exact format and include all required parameters. For instance, you do not Order and Recruit a new agent at the same time, since you need your subordinate's agent ID to Order them. You must first Recruit the agent, get the result from the system, then Order them in a separate call.\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task (you may not Order superiors, agents with the same rank as you, or agents who are not your direct reports). The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute an AWS CLI command with approval from the user. Commands must begin with aws and be well-formed. The command will be executed in the user's AWS account. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be niche or small cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed in the user's AWS account. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 5. Your Role\\n\\nYou are a Director-rank agent, the second-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs a Director-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Supervisor-rank agents, who themselves are fairly high-level actors who manage other agents. You are responsible for overseeing major operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently. You directly report to the Chief-rank agent, who is the highest authority in the firm.\\n\\nAs a #2 in the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Supervisor-rank agents.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs a Director, all of your recruitments are Supervisor-rank agents. These are fairly high-level agents who will recruit other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"executor\\\" agents who will then manage \\\"doers\\\" and middle managers down the chain of command.\\n\\n## 6. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###frontend_plan_1###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (1)\\nTo: 3\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (1) to Vader (3) with sequence ID frontend_plan_1:\\\\n\\\\n]As Director of Frontend, your first task is to produce a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) for the website’s user experience and static build pipeline.\\\\n\\\\nFunctional Pages\\\\n• Public: Home (overview), Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools.  \\\\n• Protected (login required): Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events (blog-style, CRUD via API).  \\\\n\\\\nUser Needs\\\\n• HOA admins (non-technical) must easily add News posts and upload docs.  \\\\n• Residents must self-register/login and access protected docs.  \\\\n\\\\nDEP Must Cover\\\\n1. Choice of framework (e.g., React with AWS Amplify hosting). Justify.  \\\\n2. Folder/file structure for pages/components implementing above routes with React-Router or Next.js; protected routes using Amplify Auth wrappers.  \\\\n3. Simple admin CMS approach: utilise Amplify Studio Content Manager or lightweight Netlify-CMS-style in-repo markdown—explain which and why.  \\\\n4. Build & deploy pipeline: commands to initialise Amplify project, connect GitHub repo, and push.  \\\\n5. Instructions for local dev setup, testing protected routes, and consuming API endpoints/Cognito IDs produced by backend.  \\\\n6. Accessibility & responsive design notes.  \\\\n\\\\nReturn the full DEP (including file scaffolds and CLI commands) in your report for sequence ID frontend_plan_1.\\\"\\n}\\n###frontend_plan_1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###frontend_plan_1_report###\\nSYSTEM CALL  \\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"DETERMINISTICALLY EXECUTABLE PLAN (DEP) – WEBSITE FRONTEND & STATIC BUILD PIPELINE  \\\\nSequence ID: frontend_plan_1  \\\\nAuthor: Vader (Director)\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n0. ASSUMPTIONS & SCOPE\\\\n• Execution phase will provision all AWS resources using Amplify CLI/Console only.  \\\\n• One mono-repo GitHub project named \\\\\\\"hoa-portal\\\\\\\" will hold all frontend code.  \\\\n• Backend/API/Cognito resources will be generated in a separate DEP; this plan simply consumes their outputs (user-pool-id, identity-pool-id, API endpoint names) via Amplify generated environment files.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n1. FRAMEWORK SELECTION & JUSTIFICATION\\\\nFramework: Next.js 14 (App Router, React 18) with AWS Amplify Hosting (SSG/ISR).  \\\\nJustification:  \\\\n• Out-of-box static generation for public pages, but supports server functions if required later.  \\\\n• File-system routing matches requested page list.  \\\\n• Amplify Auth & Data bindings are first-class in Next.js via @aws-amplify/ui-react-native.  \\\\n• Automatic image optimisation, built-in accessibility linter, and incremental adoption.  \\\\n• Rich plugin ecosystem (Tailwind, ESLint, Jest).  \\\\n• Easily deployable via Amplify’s Next.js SSR workflow (Amplify adds Lambda@Edge only if required; otherwise static S3 + CloudFront).  \\\\n• Supports future PWA or mobile expansion.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n2. PROJECT & FOLDER STRUCTURE  (Top-level paths)\\\\nhoa-portal/\\\\n├─ amplify/                       # auto-generated after amplify init\\\\n├─ src/\\\\n│  ├─ app/                       # Next.js App Router\\\\n│  │  ├─ layout.tsx              # Top-level layout incl. AmplifyProvider\\\\n│  │  ├─ page.tsx                # “Home” public page\\\\n│  │  ├─ contact/  page.tsx      # Contact Us\\\\n│  │  ├─ bylaws/   page.tsx      # Bylaws (public)\\\\n│  │  ├─ schools/  page.tsx      # Schools (public)\\\\n│  │  ├─ dues/      protected/   # directory for protected routes wrapper\\\\n│  │  │  └─ page.tsx             # Dues page (requires auth)\\\\n│  │  ├─ meetings/ protected/page.tsx   # Annual Meetings (protected)\\\\n│  │  ├─ blog/      protected/   # News & Events CRUD pages\\\\n│  │  │  ├─ page.tsx             # list posts\\\\n│  │  │  ├─ new/  page.tsx       # create\\\\n│  │  │  └─ [id]/  edit.tsx      # update\\\\n│  │  └─ _authGuard.tsx          # Higher-order component for route protection\\\\n│  ├─ components/\\\\n│  │  ├─ Navbar.tsx\\\\n│  │  ├─ Footer.tsx\\\\n│  │  ├─ PostCard.tsx\\\\n│  │  └─ …\\\\n│  └─ styles/  (Tailwind cfg + global.css)\\\\n├─ public/\\\\n├─ .env.local.sample             # placeholders for Amplify env vars\\\\n├─ amplifyconfiguration.json     # generated for Data/API/Auth\\\\n├─ tailwind.config.ts\\\\n├─ next.config.mjs\\\\n├─ package.json\\\\n└─ README.md\\\\n\\\\nRoute Protection\\\\n• _authGuard.tsx exports wrapper using Amplify’s withAuthenticator(App) OR custom hook `useAuthenticator` to redirect unauthenticated users. All *protected/* routes import this wrapper.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n3. ADMIN CMS APPROACH\\\\nChoice: Amplify Studio Content Manager + Data models.\\\\nRationale:  \\\\n• Non-technical HOA admins obtain a web GUI (Studio) to add/edit \\\\\\\"Post\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"Document\\\\\\\" items without touching Git.  \\\\n• Permissions tied to Cognito groups; avoids Netlify-CMS markdown merge conflicts.  \\\\n• Real-time GraphQL DataStore sync to frontend with minimal code.  \\\\n• Removes need for maintaining separate admin build or markdown parsing.\\\\nImplementation Steps (Execution phase reference):  \\\\n1. `amplify add api` → GraphQL schema with Post, Document types.  \\\\n2. `amplify pull --appId <id>` generates types/models used in components.  \\\\n3. In Amplify Studio → Enable Content, set Post & Document collections, attach IAM/Cognito auth rules.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n4. BUILD & DEPLOY PIPELINE (CLI commands – deterministic)\\\\nRun locally from project root unless stated.\\\\nStep A – Initialise repository\\\\n1. `npx create-next-app@latest hoa-portal --typescript --eslint --tailwind --use-npm --no-src-dir=false`\\\\n2. `cd hoa-portal && git init && git remote add origin git@github.com:<ORG>/hoa-portal.git && git add . && git commit -m \\\\\\\"chore(init)\\\\\\\" && git push -u origin main`\\\\n\\\\nStep B – Amplify project\\\\n3. `npm install aws-amplify @aws-amplify/ui-react`  \\\\n4. `amplify init --appId <auto-generated> --envName dev --yes`  \\\\n   Deterministic answers:  \\\\n   • name: hoa-portal  \\\\n   • env: dev  \\\\n   • editor: Visual Studio Code  \\\\n   • type: javascript  \\\\n   • framework: react  \\\\n   • src: src  \\\\n   • build: npm run build  \\\\n   • start: npm run dev  \\\\n   • use AWS profile: default\\\\n5. `amplify add hosting` (Amplify-Managed, prod, `./out` folder) → select *Continuous deployment (GitHub)*, branch `main`.\\\\n6. `amplify push --yes`  # provisions hosting bucket, CloudFront, etc.\\\\n7. Verify Amplify Console shows CI pipeline linked to GitHub; first deploy auto-runs.\\\\n\\\\nStep C – Branch previews (optional deterministic)\\\\n8. `git checkout -b feature/x && git push --set-upstream origin feature/x` → Amplify auto-creates preview URL.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n5. LOCAL DEV, TESTING & API CONSUMPTION\\\\nSetup\\\\n• Prereqs: Node 20, npm 10, AWS CLI v2, Amplify CLI 12+.  \\\\n• `git clone git@github.com:<ORG>/hoa-portal.git && cd hoa-portal && npm install`\\\\n• Obtain backend environment from Amplify: `amplify pull --envName dev --yes`\\\\n• Copy `.env.local.sample` → `.env.local`; no secrets required because Amplify configuration file handles it.\\\\nRun\\\\n• `npm run dev` (Next.js on http://localhost:3000)\\\\n• Sign-up path automatically renders Amplify Authenticator component.\\\\nTesting Protected Routes deterministically\\\\n1. Navigate to `/dues` while signed out → should redirect to `/login` from _authGuard.  \\\\n2. Sign-up as new resident; confirm email; access `/dues` successfully.  \\\\n3. For admin CMS, add user to Cognito group `admins` via AWS Console; login, navigate `/blog/new` → form rendered.\\\\nAPI Consumption\\\\n• Post CRUD uses Amplify DataStore or GraphQL client:  \\\\n```ts\\\\nimport { createPost } from \\\\\\\"graphql/mutations\\\\\\\"; import { API, graphqlOperation } from \\\\\\\"aws-amplify\\\\\\\";\\\\nawait API.graphql(graphqlOperation(createPost, { input: { title, content, author } }));\\\\n```\\\\n• Auth headers handled automatically by Amplify Auth.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n6. ACCESSIBILITY & RESPONSIVE DESIGN\\\\n• Use semantic elements (<header>, <nav>, <main>, <article>) and Next.js built-in <Image> for alt tags.  \\\\n• Enforce WCAG 2.1 AA colour contrast via Tailwind config; run `npm run lint` (eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y).  \\\\n• Keyboard navigation: Amplify Authenticator already handles focus traps; custom components tested with `@testing-library/user-event`.\\\\n• Mobile-first Tailwind classes, CSS grid or flex for breakpoint-based layout (sm/md/lg).  \\\\n• ARIA live regions for form submission states in admin CMS.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n7. DEP COMPLETION CRITERIA\\\\n• After Execution: GitHub main branch push successfully builds & deploys; /home reachable.  \\\\n• Amplify Studio shows Post & Document collections with CRUD access limited to Cognito group `admins`.  \\\\n• Visiting protected path while unauthenticated redirects; authenticated users in proper group succeed.  \\\\n• Lighthouse audit ≥ 90 for accessibility & performance on mobile.\\\\n\\\\nEND OF DEP\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"frontend_plan_1\\\"\\n}\\n###frontend_plan_1_report###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###frontend_rev_2###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (1)\\nTo: 3\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (1) to Vader (3) with sequence ID frontend_rev_2:\\\\n\\\\n]Partagaz D flagged lack of a truly non-technical CMS/admin experience. Revise your DEP with the following additions (delta only):\\\\n\\\\n1. ADMIN PORTAL & CMS\\\\n   • Add /admin route (protected by Cognito group \\\\\\\"admins\\\\\\\") presenting a dashboard with cards for:\\\\n       – \\\\\\\"News & Events\\\\\\\" (list, create, edit, delete posts via Amplify DataStore GraphQL mutations).\\\\n       – \\\\\\\"Documents\\\\\\\" (upload PDF to S3 protected path via Amplify Storage.put; surface metadata via GraphQL Document model).\\\\n       – \\\\\\\"Residents\\\\\\\" (link opens Amplify Admin UI › User management in a new tab).\\\\n   • For News & Events editing, integrate a rich-text WYSIWYG ( @tinymce/tinymce-react ) component so content is edited visually—no Markdown required.\\\\n\\\\n2. WYSIWYG & FORMS IMPLEMENTATION\\\\n   • Modify Post model to include `contentHtml` string field to store TinyMCE output.\\\\n   • Provide React component snippets (max 1 file each) for NewsEditor.tsx and DocsUploader.tsx; include amplification of Storage.put and creating Document entry.\\\\n\\\\n3. AMPLIFY STUDIO CONTENT MANAGER\\\\n   • Document the one-time steps to enable Studio → Content → Post & Document collections, grant admins group access, and share invitation links with board members.\\\\n   • Explain how Studio gives a GUI for non-technical edits in parallel to the in-app admin dashboard (either path acceptable).\\\\n\\\\n4. UX POLISH\\\\n   • Add quick-links in Navbar for logged-in admins to reach dashboard.\\\\n   • Ensure protected pages display friendly \\\\\\\"You must log in\\\\\\\" message instead of blank redirect.\\\\n\\\\n5. UPDATE DEP SECTIONS\\\\n   • Provide revised folder structure and any new Amplify commands (`amplify add storage`, `amplify push`) but only list the deltas—not the full earlier DEP.\\\\n   • Update accessibility statement for new components.\\\\n\\\\nReturn your delta-focused DEP update in report sequencing frontend_rev_2.\\\"\\n}\\n###frontend_rev_2###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###frontend_rev_2_report###\\nSYSTEM CALL  \\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"DELTA UPDATE – DEP FRONTEND (NON-TECHNICAL CMS EXPERIENCE)\\\\nSequence ID: frontend_rev_2  \\\\nAuthor: Vader (Director)\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\nΔ1. ADMIN PORTAL & CMS ADDITION\\\\n• New protected route: /admin  (Next.js src/app/admin/protected/page.tsx). Guarded by _authGuard plus Cognito group check: user.signInUserSession.idToken.payload[\\\\\\\"cognito:groups\\\\\\\"] includes \\\\\\\"admins\\\\\\\".  \\\\n• AdminDashboard.tsx displays three cards (News & Events, Documents, Residents).   \\\\n   – News & Events → /admin/news  (list + NewsEditor).  \\\\n   – Documents → /admin/documents (DocsUploader + list).  \\\\n   – Residents → external link: Amplify Admin UI › User management (`https://<appId>.admin.amplifyapp.com/user-management`). Opens in new tab.  \\\\n• Friendly message for non-admin authenticated users: “You need administrator rights to access this page.”\\\\n\\\\nΔ2. WYSIWYG & FORMS IMPLEMENTATION\\\\nGraphQL Post model amendment (amplify/backend/api/schema.graphql):\\\\n```\\\\ntype Post @model @auth(rules:[{allow: groups, groups:[\\\\\\\"admins\\\\\\\"], operations:[create,update,delete,read]},{allow: public, operations:[read]}]) {\\\\n  id: ID!\\\\n  title: String!\\\\n  contentHtml: String!    # TinyMCE output stored as HTML\\\\n  createdAt: AWSDateTime\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\nRun deterministically:\\\\n`amplify api gql-compile`  (auto–executed inside `amplify push`)\\\\n\\\\nComponent Snippets  (1 file each – placed in src/components/)\\\\n--- NewsEditor.tsx ---\\\\n```tsx\\\\n\\\\\\\"use client\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { useState } from \\\\\\\"react\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { Editor } from \\\\\\\"@tinymce/tinymce-react\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { API, graphqlOperation } from \\\\\\\"aws-amplify\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { createPost, updatePost } from \\\\\\\"graphql/mutations\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { Post } from \\\\\\\"../models\\\\\\\";\\\\n\\\\nexport default function NewsEditor({ existing }: { existing?: Post }) {\\\\n  const [title, setTitle] = useState(existing?.title ?? \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");\\\\n  const [contentHtml, setContentHtml] = useState(existing?.contentHtml ?? \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");\\\\n  const save = async () => {\\\\n    const input = { id: existing?.id, title, contentHtml };\\\\n    const mutation = existing ? updatePost : createPost;\\\\n    await API.graphql(graphqlOperation(mutation, { input }));\\\\n    alert(\\\\\\\"Saved\\\\\\\");\\\\n  };\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div className=\\\\\\\"space-y-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <input\\\\n        aria-label=\\\\\\\"Post title\\\\\\\"\\\\n        className=\\\\\\\"w-full border p-2\\\\\\\"\\\\n        value={title}\\\\n        onChange={e => setTitle(e.target.value)}\\\\n        placeholder=\\\\\\\"Title\\\\\\\"\\\\n      />\\\\n      <Editor\\\\n        apiKey=\\\\\\\"no-api-key\\\\\\\"\\\\n        value={contentHtml}\\\\n        onEditorChange={setContentHtml}\\\\n        init={{ menubar: false, height: 300, toolbar_mode: \\\\\\\"wrap\\\\\\\" }}\\\\n      />\\\\n      <button className=\\\\\\\"btn-primary\\\\\\\" onClick={save} aria-label=\\\\\\\"Save post\\\\\\\">Save</button>\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n--- DocsUploader.tsx ---\\\\n```tsx\\\\n\\\\\\\"use client\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { useState } from \\\\\\\"react\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { Storage, API, graphqlOperation } from \\\\\\\"aws-amplify\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { createDocument } from \\\\\\\"graphql/mutations\\\\\\\";\\\\n\\\\nexport default function DocsUploader() {\\\\n  const [file, setFile] = useState<File | null>(null);\\\\n  const upload = async () => {\\\\n    if (!file) return;\\\\n    const key = `protected/${file.name}`;\\\\n    await Storage.put(key, file, { contentType: file.type, level: \\\\\\\"protected\\\\\\\" });\\\\n    await API.graphql(graphqlOperation(createDocument, {\\\\n      input: { name: file.name, s3Key: key, uploadedAt: new Date().toISOString() }\\\\n    }));\\\\n    alert(\\\\\\\"Uploaded\\\\\\\");\\\\n  };\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div className=\\\\\\\"space-y-2\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <input type=\\\\\\\"file\\\\\\\" accept=\\\\\\\"application/pdf\\\\\\\" onChange={e => setFile(e.target.files?.[0] ?? null)} />\\\\n      <button className=\\\\\\\"btn-primary\\\\\\\" onClick={upload} aria-label=\\\\\\\"Upload document\\\\\\\">Upload</button>\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nΔ3. AMPLIFY STUDIO CONTENT MANAGER – ONE-TIME STEPS\\\\n1. `amplify console --appId <appId>` → open Amplify Console, click “Launch Studio”.  \\\\n2. In Studio, enable “Content”. Two collections auto-detected from schema: Post, Document.  \\\\n3. Grant access: Settings → Access management → Add Cognito group → `admins` (CRUD).  \\\\n4. Invite board members: Users → Invite → enter emails; Studio sends magic-link with limited content access.  \\\\n5. Editors can now add Posts/Docs via browser GUI without logging into GitHub or app; changes sync through DataStore instantly, parallel to in-app admin dashboard.\\\\n\\\\nΔ4. UX POLISH\\\\n• Navbar.tsx now conditionally renders “Dashboard” link when `groups?.includes(\\\\\\\"admins\\\\\\\")`.  \\\\n• _authGuard.tsx updated: if unauthenticated → render centered message “Please log in to access this page” with `<Authenticator/>` component fallback, instead of silent redirect.  \\\\n• For colour-blind admins, dashboard card icons include text labels.\\\\n\